          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "**If you have any of these, seek emergency care now:** vomiting blood; vomit that looks like coffee grounds; black, tarry, foul-smelling stools; any bleeding with dizziness, faintness, collapse, a racing heartbeat, cold clammy skin, confusion, or breathlessness; or chest pain, pressure, or discomfort. A history of reflux does not rule out a heart attack, and heartburn-like discomfort can be a heart attack, so do not assume it is reflux.\n\nGuidance on reflux (GERD) lists seven alarm features that change the plan. For each, the guideline says to ask a clinician promptly for endoscopy (a camera test of the food pipe and stomach) rather than a trial of reflux treatment. The features are difficulty swallowing or pain on swallowing, unintentional weight loss, iron-deficiency anaemia, persistent vomiting, a lump you can feel in the abdomen or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer, and new symptoms starting at an older age. Gut bleeding is also on the list, and it is covered by the emergency advice above. The guideline gives the same action for all of these and does not rank them by urgency. It says \"prompt\" but does not say whether that means the same day or within weeks, so ask your clinician how soon you should be seen.\n\nOne specialist said that persistent vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down needs urgent assessment. The urgency framing for these features comes from the diagnostic guidance, which places persistent vomiting among the features for prompt clinician review. If you cannot keep fluids down, contact a clinician or local urgent service today rather than waiting. The retrieved material found no other disagreement between authorities, but it contained only one reflux guideline, and it does not say how strong the evidence is for each alarm feature. A clinician or a fuller guideline review can check both points.\n\nI have not assessed any symptoms of your own, because your question was general. If you describe what you are experiencing, I can run a risk check on it. If you are unsure, contact a clinician or local urgent service today.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
